BT /F2 17 Tf 56 736 Td (Pointer to pointer) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 56 702 Td 15 TL
(A pointer to a pointer stores the address of another pointer. It is declared with two stars,) Tj T*
(as in int **pp. If p is an int * and pp = &p, then *pp is p and **pp is the int that p) Tj T*
(points to.) Tj T*
T*
(Why use one? Because C passes arguments by value, a function that must change a pointer, for) Tj T*
(example to make it point to newly allocated memory, needs the address of that pointer, which) Tj T*
(is a pointer to a pointer.) Tj T*
T*
(The main function uses it too: in int main\(int argc, char **argv\), argv is a pointer to the) Tj T*
(first element of an array of char pointers, one for each command line argument.) Tj T*
T*
(A dynamic two-dimensional array is built the same way: allocate an array of row pointers,) Tj T*
(then allocate each row, and keep the whole thing in an int ** variable.) Tj T*
ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 56 40 Td (Introduction to C Pointers: Study Notes \(sample document\)  -  Page 3 of 5) Tj ET
